# Function & variable design

## Meaningful names

- Function and variable names must always be **intention-revealing** — say what they do or hold.
- Avoid abbreviations, single-letter names (except loop indices), and vague names (`data`,
  `temp`, `helper`, `handle`, `doStuff`).
- Prefer verbs for functions (`loadProjectById`) and nouns for values (`projectId`).

```js
// ❌ BAD
const d = await get(id);
function h(r, s) { /* ... */ }

 // ✅ GOOD
const project = await loadProjectById(projectId);
async function listActiveProjects(organizationId) { /* ... */ }
```

## Own the response — never hand off `res`

A function that receives the HTTP response (`res` / `Reply`) **must not pass it** to another
function. The caller that owns the request/response cycle is the only place that writes the
response (`res.status(…).json(…)`, `reply.send(…)`, etc.).

Helpers return data or throw; they never call `res.*`.

```js
// ❌ BAD — control of `res` transferred to a helper
async function sendProject(res, projectId) {
  const project = await loadProjectById(projectId);
  res.json(project);
}

app.get('/projects/:id', async (req, res) => {
  await sendProject(res, req.params.id);
});

// ✅ GOOD — handler owns `res`; helper returns data
app.get('/projects/:id', async (req, res) => {
  const project = await loadProjectById(req.params.id);
  res.json(project);
});
```

## Always use Promises

- Prefer **`async`/`await`** (Promise-based) for all asynchronous work.
- Do not introduce callback-style APIs for new code.
- If a library is callback-only, wrap it once at the boundary with `util.promisify` or a thin
  Promise adapter, then `await` everywhere else.

```js
// ❌ BAD
fs.readFile(path, (err, data) => { /* ... */ });

// ✅ GOOD
const data = await fs.promises.readFile(path);
```

## Extract functions only when reused

- Do **not** create a function for one or two lines used in a single place.
- Inline small, one-off logic at the call site.
- Extract a named function only when the same logic is (or clearly will be) used in
  **two or more** places, or when the body is complex enough that a name materially aids
  understanding (multi-branch / multi-step domain logic — not a one-liner wrapper).

```js
// ❌ BAD — one-liner helper used once
function toStatusCode(ok) {
  return ok ? 200 : 400;
}
res.status(toStatusCode(valid)).json(body);

// ✅ GOOD — inline at the single call site
res.status(valid ? 200 : 400).json(body);

// ✅ GOOD — shared logic used in multiple handlers
function mapProjectToDto(project) { /* ... */ }
```
